    <article class="prose">
      <p class="meta">Single-cell genomics · Medulloblastoma</p>
      <h2>Subgroup-specific regulatory circuits in medulloblastoma</h2>
      <p class="lede-small">Advanced Science, 2024 · Co-first author, listed first</p>
      <h3>The question and constraint</h3>
      <p>The project initially aimed to connect medulloblastoma subgroups with stages or cell populations in normal brain development. That question required suitable normal human developmental reference data, which were difficult to obtain, and known developmental markers did not reveal a sufficiently clear pattern in the available samples.</p>
      <h3>How the question changed</h3>
      <p>Before asking where each tumor originated, the samples first needed reliable molecular subgroup assignments. I reviewed published subgroup markers, classified the samples using chromatin-derived gene activity, and then examined subgroup-specific accessibility and regulatory programs.</p>
      <h3>My contribution</h3>
      <p>I performed the primary scATAC-seq computational analyses, including quality assessment, dimensionality reduction, clustering, annotation, differential accessibility, motif analysis, and regulatory network construction. When no single workflow directly produced the needed network, I reviewed published methods and integrated multiple outputs into a regulon-like analysis. I also contributed to manuscript writing and computational responses during peer review.</p>
      <p>I separately explored public scRNA-seq datasets in Seurat for independent transcriptional evidence. The available data did not provide sufficiently suitable support, so these analyses were not used to extend the final conclusions.</p>
      <h3>Collaborative contributions and outcome</h3>
      <p>Clinical collaborators provided samples and clinical interpretation, and contributed supporting bulk data, experimental validation, and the extension toward neurotransmitter receptor biology. The study analyzed chromatin accessibility across 59,015 cells and identified subgroup-specific regulatory elements and TF–NTR circuits.</p>
      <div class="link-row"><a class="button-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.202309554">Read publication</a><a class="text-link" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38884167/">View on PubMed</a></div>
    </article>
  </section>

