    <article class="prose">
      <p class="meta">Cancer epigenetics · B-cell lymphoma</p>
      <h2>Investigating the tumor-suppressive role of DNMT3B</h2>
      <p class="lede-small">Ongoing doctoral research</p>
      <h3>Starting point</h3>
      <p>The project began with knockout cell lines and sequencing datasets generated by a previous doctoral researcher. During my first year, I followed an existing hypothesis based primarily on ChIP-seq results, but progress along that direction was limited.</p>
      <h3>Turning point and contribution</h3>
      <p>I returned to the sequencing data and reconsidered the RNA-seq results alongside the ChIP-seq analysis. This suggested an alternative interpretation and led me to propose a new direction. I performed the computational reanalysis, selected an initial functional assay, and carried out the primary validation work. After the initial result supported the hypothesis, the project expanded to additional lymphoma cell lines and collaborative mouse studies.</p>
      <h3>Collaboration and current challenge</h3>
      <p>My advisor and a senior lab member recommended validation in multiple cell lines. The senior researcher also provided substantial help with mouse-study design and hands-on training. The project has established a reproducible phenotype, while the intermediate mechanism remains unresolved.</p>
      <p class="note">Only findings disclosed in public abstracts and conference materials are presented here.</p>
      <h3>Recognition</h3>
      <ul class="compact-list"><li>2025 ASH Graduate Hematology Award</li><li>2025 ASH Abstract Achievement Award</li><li>Poster presentation at the 67th ASH Annual Meeting</li><li>Poster presentation at the 2025 UW Carbone Research Retreat</li></ul>
